<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bibliothèque nationale de France</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as National Library of France</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bibliothèque nationale de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is the national library of France in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, which grew from the royal library of the French kings. It holds one of the world's largest collections of books, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, maps, coins and prints. Among its treasures is the only surviving volume of the Korean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jikji">Jikji</a> of 1377, the oldest known book printed with movable metal type.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france.jpg" alt="Bibliothèque nationale de France" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur Weidmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biblioth%C3%A8que_Mitterrand_Mai_2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The library traces its origin to the royal library founded at the Louvre Palace by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1368, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> he had received from his predecessor John II. The first librarian of record was Claude Mallet, the king's valet de chambre, who made a catalogue of the books. After the death of Charles VI the collection was bought by the English regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Duke of Bedford, who took it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1424, and it was apparently dispersed at his death in 1435.</p><p>The invention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> led to a new collection at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> in 1461. Later kings added to it: Charles VIII seized part of the collection of the kings of Aragon, Louis XII brought in the library at Blois, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> merged it with his private library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> in 1534.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The collection moved several times in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and many treasures were lost. Under the librarian Jacques Auguste de Thou it became the largest and richest collection of books in the world, and it grew rapidly under Louis XIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, in great part through the interest of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>. The library moved in 1666 to two houses in the Rue Vivienne and opened to the public in 1692. Jean-Paul Bignon reformed its system, and from 1721 the Richelieu site, the main location for 275 years, began to take shape.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> was established in September 1792 the Assembly declared the Bibliothèque du Roi national property and it became the Bibliothèque Nationale. Its collections passed 300,000 volumes as the private libraries of aristocrats and clergy were seized. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ordered provincial libraries to send it any book it lacked and added spoil from his conquests, some of it returned after his fall. In 1868 it moved into new buildings on the Rue de Richelieu designed by Henri Labrouste, and in 1896 it was still the largest repository of books in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The library is a public establishment under the Ministry of Culture and the national repository of all that is published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which by law must be deposited there. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> announced a new library on 14 July 1988; the architect Dominique Perrault designed it, and after cost overruns that earned it the nickname Très Grande Bibliothèque it was inaugurated on 15 December 1996.</p><p>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> department houses the largest collection of medieval and modern manuscripts worldwide, including about 5,000 Ancient Greek manuscripts. Gallica, its digital library established in October 1997, makes freely available millions of books, manuscripts, newspapers, images, maps and recordings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bibliothèque nationale de France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jikji, printed at Heungdeoksa temple in Cheongju in 1377, is the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type; its only surviving volume is in the <b>National Library of France</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The most famous manuscript, of the Syrian branch and written between 1450 and 1593, holds 281 nights and about 36 tales; Galland acquired it, and it is now in the <b>Bibliothèque nationale de France</b> in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Its claimed origin is a Latin manuscript of 1287 in the <b>Bibliothèque nationale de France</b>: a poem in it has two verses close to two of the later song's, though neither the first verse nor the words of the title appear, and its music has nothing to do…</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur</a></li><li><blockquote>…1966 the British musicologist Mary Berry, an Augustinian canoness writing as Mother Thomas More, found the melody in a 15th-century French processional of chants for burials in the <b>National Library of France</b>, where it carries another text, &quot;Bone Jesu dulcis cunctis&quot;, as part of a series of tropes.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a></li></ul></section>
